WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
Film Courage: How many people were involved&nbsp;
in starting your production company?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:05.680
Now, how many people are involved?

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:09.920
Van Ditthavong, Filmmaker/Photographer:&nbsp;
When I started it was me and my wife.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:22.960
Now it’s me with…one…two…four other&nbsp;
folks and a whole bunch of interns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:34.160
and a roster of really great creatives&nbsp;
and directors. It’s become a little group.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:50.000
I’m very fortunate to find teammates who want&nbsp;
to go through the battlefield of being part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:56.960
a production company. We started out small like&nbsp;
anyone just sitting out there How do people do it?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:03.280
What do you have to do. So I just started&nbsp;
and I didn’t worry about the equipment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:12.240
If there was a bigger job to do we would rent it.&nbsp;
I didn’t want to buy 20, 30, 40,000 dollars worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.440
of equipment and have it sit there for a year or&nbsp;
two and let it depreciate and not get its value.

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:34.400
Everything has taken a hit now with 2020. We also&nbsp;
had a sales rep that represents goPOP FILMS and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:44.400
directors in the midwest and southeast. They pitch&nbsp;
the ad agencies and art directors on our roster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:58.960
If there are treatments or spots or things&nbsp;
that we could bid on. We also go direct to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:09.280
brand or direct to companies that need video&nbsp;
content. But at the moment obviously production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:21.920
is picking up but you want to be safe. That’s the&nbsp;
key, low overhead so you can maintain and sustain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:30.480
so when the economy comes up you will be able to&nbsp;
position yourself. If we think about it right now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:45.840
as we are in preproduction, so when production&nbsp;
comes we are ready to go being ready to service&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:52.160
our clients to the best of our ability. I think&nbsp;
that’s where we are at right now in the 2020 era.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:57.280
Film Courage: Right and with other&nbsp;
booms and busts with say the DotCom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:04.560
when things were really good in&nbsp;
the 1990’s…can you talk about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:09.760
not getting too confident knowing that there&nbsp;
will be a winter, there is always a winter.

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:22.480
Van: Yes, the life cycle of things.&nbsp;
You just have to be steady and know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:30.640
especially with this you might have a lot of&nbsp;
clients or maybe your business has one big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:38.240
client but what if they disappear? What if your&nbsp;
client was a lot of the travel industry right now?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:48.160
But what if you weren’t busy, you also have&nbsp;
a plastic company and they’re needing a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:56.240
of things so it just all depends where your&nbsp;
position in the market changes. You just have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:09.360
be…what is the saying? Expect the worse, prepare&nbsp;
for the best. It’s one of those things where you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:18.080
can’t be a fat cat, you just keep going&nbsp;
because there will be lean times and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:24.960
same with films when films come out. We just&nbsp;
released our film [ALL ROADS TO PEARLA]. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:30.480
don’t know where the market, that’s the&nbsp;
toughest thing about filmmaking because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:36.400
you have the thing that you made that you started&nbsp;
3, 4, 10 years ago but when it reaches market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:43.520
is it the right time for this film? Does this&nbsp;
touch a nerve? Is it 2 years too early, 2 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:49.440
too late? So it’s the same when you release your&nbsp;
product. Is it right or not? Maybe it catches on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:55.760
later. What was that with DONNIE DARKO, it wasn’t&nbsp;
so hot here in The States, it goes international,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:03.280
comes back, it’s a five year process. It happens&nbsp;
with a lot of different things. For anyone who has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:11.920
had a short film or other film I think you&nbsp;
go back and focus on that creating because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:18.640
you can’t determine the market but you can prepare&nbsp;
for it. I think that's the balance you have to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:28.400
Be prepared for lean times and that&nbsp;
would be the best way to go about it.

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:32.160
Film Courage: What area of business&nbsp;
does your production company focus on?

00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:43.360
Van: We have a lot of different directors whether&nbsp;
it’s healthcare, food, fashion, documentaries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:54.240
We cover a lot of different industries, we can&nbsp;
tackle them all. I figured this out because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:00.320
when I was a director trying to&nbsp;
get into a production company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:08.560
I wasn’t going to get hired for a comedy spot&nbsp;
because my reel or my work isn’t comedic. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:17.440
wasn’t going to get hired for food because&nbsp;
I didn’t do any food. I had a documentary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:23.760
feel. Those are the different jobs&nbsp;
that I would get (more cinematic). So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:34.880
I thought we will have our company instead of&nbsp;
turning down jobs, let’s find the best directors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:43.760
who does food, who does cars, who does fashion,&nbsp;
who does travel, and that’s your roster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:54.960
If you’re a client who needs cinematic images that&nbsp;
need to have your brand or message elevated and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.960 --> 00:07:04.800
tell a story, that is what we do. We want to help&nbsp;
you tell your story. That's our selling point.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:12.560
Bringing cinematic imagery and production to&nbsp;
elevate your brand and your company’s message.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:22.880
So it could be the wine industry, it could&nbsp;
be fashion, we pretty much tackle it.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:26.160
Film Courage: How do you know these&nbsp;
directors are a fit for your company?

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:38.160
Van: Sure, so when we see the director’s work&nbsp;
and it has to be a certain level and certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:48.240
voice that they’re telling. Second, I&nbsp;
meet with them and if they have a certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:55.440
philosophy or personality because we don’t want to&nbsp;
work with jerks, there are already so many walking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.440 --> 00:08:06.080
around. We want to work with good people who are&nbsp;
focused on producing really good work and also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:16.720
are nice. We have this motto of “Work hard and&nbsp;
be nice.” Of that aspect so the directors not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.720 --> 00:08:22.400
only have to be talented but just have to be nice&nbsp;
people because when we are on set and when you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:30.800
dealing with clients, who wants a temperamental&nbsp;
director? It’s like we should be fortunate to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:38.640
working for you. I want to be surrounded by people&nbsp;
of that same attitude of like how can we help you?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:50.560
It’s like business…you can’t take anything&nbsp;
for granted. Every little job, every big job,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:57.840
it’s a job. It should be treated the same,&nbsp;
highly valuable. I remember Neiman Marcus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:07.360
someone was telling me the sales training or&nbsp;
someone’s training as you treat everyone who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:12.080
walks in the door the same. Someone who can&nbsp;
look like their transient versus someone who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.080 --> 00:09:19.040
has on a mink coat. You never know who is who. So&nbsp;
that’s the same thing with an email, with a call,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:27.360
with your client base. We are just fortunate to&nbsp;
be able to see if we can help you. That’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:33.200
type of directors and if they have that attitude&nbsp;
and they want to put their nose to the grind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:39.360
and then they do. Funny enough, a lot of our&nbsp;
directors have a background in photography&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:44.960
(similar to me) so they are actually starting&nbsp;
out as photographers too so it's nice to have.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:54.000
I know visually that they could capture what&nbsp;
they want. They could actually act as their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:57.840
DP [director of photography] or&nbsp;
work with DPs or things like that.

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:02.000
Funny enough a lot of our&nbsp;
directors are shooters themselves.

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:07.200
Film Courage: And want’s interesting sometimes,&nbsp;
just like you said the mink coast versus the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:14.880
transient analogy sometimes in Los Angeles the&nbsp;
mink coat…the more show…the less someone has.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:19.120
That’s the weird part of being here and maybe&nbsp;
this is the same in New York City too but a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:28.160
of times that’s just a front and is not real.
Van: Exactly, you just never know and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:36.000
so those are the types of directors or graphic&nbsp;
designers or animators or people who want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:44.080
work with, even our crew. They always say&nbsp;
it starts from the top and hopefully we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:56.080
create a culture that is professional but also&nbsp;
creates that atmosphere where your voice is heard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:06.560
No one is above anyone else. Everyone is open&nbsp;
to suggestions and everyone is artistic but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:11.680
we don’t need to be Van Gogh here and&nbsp;
be all crazy if you don’t get your way.

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:19.600
Film Courage: What are your day-to-day duties with&nbsp;
your company maybe pre-2020 and now during 2020?

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:29.120
Van: You get up, you fire up that laptop or&nbsp;
that desktop and you go through your emails.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:41.840
A lot right now “Let’s talk” since it’s&nbsp;
2020, research (a lot of research going on).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.640 --> 00:11:55.200
In terms of new clients that we want to work with&nbsp;
or we’re in a new industry, we have a new industry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.200 --> 00:12:02.560
that we’re trying to penetrate is the golf and&nbsp;
luxury resort industry prior to 2020. We’ve got a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:10.560
couple of great directors who do that so we&nbsp;
are trying to match that up. Now that’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:23.600
commercial side but for the film side I have a&nbsp;
couple of different films in development right now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.600 --> 00:12:36.080
and one is we’re shaping up a script to go out&nbsp;
for attachments and things like that so the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:46.720
couple of months I’ve been trying to get&nbsp;
that script right. We are also producing this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:53.920
feature film for this Dutch filmmaker&nbsp;
Yfke van Berckelaer (her first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.040 --> 00:13:02.080
feature film). Her short film LILY has&nbsp;
been screened in over 50 film festivals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.080 --> 00:13:08.560
around the world. It’s a Me Too horror film.&nbsp;
And Phosphor one of her first feature films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:13.600
we are fortunate enough to be producing that so&nbsp;
we’re trying to get things lined up for for that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:19.360
Like we were talking about before there are&nbsp;
still a lot of things that could be done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:24.720
even though everyone is getting used to Zoom and&nbsp;
all this stuff but it is laying the seeds and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:29.040
getting all that done. That takes most of the day.

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:38.960
We just released the film [ALL ROADS&nbsp;
TO PEARLA] to weeks ago September 25th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.680 --> 00:13:48.880
last month so we’re still pushing that as well.&nbsp;
Lots of little different things on our plate.

00:13:50.080 --> 00:13:52.960
Film Courage: Was pre-2020 responding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:59.280
and post-2020 or during 2020&nbsp;
is now hunting, doing research?

00:13:59.280 --> 00:14:06.240
Van: Yes, hunting, doing research&nbsp;
because you don’t know who is going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:13.840
left standing. It’s a weird thing. We&nbsp;
were talking with our sales agent where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.680 --> 00:14:21.680
we’ve released our film for North America&nbsp;
but we are still entertaining international&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:30.160
territories like Germany, UK, Ireland and all&nbsp;
of these other parts but because of 2020 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:36.880
different things companies have gone under so&nbsp;
it’s almost like everyone is waiting to see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:48.800
what is left, who is standing. Everyone is&nbsp;
adjusting so you kind of have to take an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:58.960
optimistic point and do your research&nbsp;
and tread water until there is a break.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:10.800
It’s very interesting, for commercial&nbsp;
work we had a lot of shoots postponed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:22.320
canceled things like that so you deal with&nbsp;
the blows, you take it on the chin but you’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:39.840
got to still stand up and&nbsp;
prepare as when things open up.

00:15:39.840 --> 00:15:43.360
Question For The Viewers: What part&nbsp;
of this video was most helpful to you?

